    "body": "`MonetrixAccountant.totalBackingSigned()` counts Portfolio Margin borrow/lend supplied balances from Hyperliquid precompile `0x811`, but it never subtracts the corresponding borrow liabilities exposed by the same precompile. As a result, PM collateral can be reported as backing while PM debt is omitted.\n\nThe inflated backing flows into `surplus()`, `distributableSurplus()`, and Gate 3 of `settleDailyPnL`. The Operator can then settle and distribute yield that does not exist economically. After distribution, Monetrix can still report full solvency, while real backing is lower than USDM supply by the omitted PM borrow debt.\n\n## Finding Description\n\nThe root cause is in `PrecompileReader.suppliedBalance()`. Hyperliquid's `0x811` borrow/lend user-state precompile returns a full `BorrowLendUserTokenState`:\n\n```solidity\nstruct BasisAndValue {\n  uint64 basis;\n  uint64 value;\n}\n\nstruct BorrowLendUserTokenState {\n  BasisAndValue borrow;\n  BasisAndValue supply;\n}\n```\n\nThis ABI is attached by Hyperliquid as `L1Read.sol` from the official HyperCore interaction docs. In ABI form, the response is four `uint64` words:\n\n```text\nborrow.basis, borrow.value, supply.basis, supply.value\n```\n\nHyperliquid's public API docs expose the same model for `borrowLendUserState`, with per-token `borrow` and `supply` objects. Hyperliquid's Portfolio Margin docs also state that insufficient balances can be automatically borrowed against eligible collateral, and borrowed assets accrue interest.\n\nMonetrix decodes the same response but discards the first three words and returns only the fourth word, `supply.value`:\n\n[`src/core/PrecompileReader.sol#L84-L91`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2026-04-monetrix/blob/main/src/core/PrecompileReader.sol#L84-L91)\n\n```solidity\n/// @notice PM \"supplied\" balance (0x811). Returned in L1 8-dp wei.\nfunction suppliedBalance(address account, uint64 token) internal view returns (uint64 supplied) {\n    (bool ok, bytes memory res) = HyperCoreConstants.PRECOMPILE_SUPPLIED_BALANCE.staticcall(\n        abi.encode(account, token)\n    );\n    require(ok && res.length >= 128, \"PrecompileReader: supplied balance read failed\");\n    (,,, supplied) = abi.decode(res, (uint64, uint64, uint64, uint64));\n}\n```\n\n`MonetrixAccountant._readL1Backing()` then adds registered supplied values to backing:\n\n[`src/core/MonetrixAccountant.sol#L146-L157`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2026-04-monetrix/blob/main/src/core/MonetrixAccountant.sol#L146-L157)\n\n```solidity\nuint256 slen = suppliedList.length;\nfor (uint256 i = 0; i < slen; i++) {\n    SuppliedAsset storage a = suppliedList[i];\n    if (a.spotToken == uint64(HyperCoreConstants.USDC_TOKEN_INDEX)) {\n        total += int256(PrecompileReader.suppliedUsdcEvm(account));\n    } else {\n        total += int256(\n            PrecompileReader.suppliedNotionalUsdcFromPerp(uint32(a.spotToken), a.perpIndex, account)\n        );\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThere is no corresponding read of `borrow.value`, no conversion of borrowed non-USDC assets to USDC, and no subtraction from `total`.\n\nThis directly breaks the backing model. The same PM account can have:\n\n1. supplied BTC worth 1,000 USDC; and\n2. borrowed USDC worth 1,000 USDC.\n\nEconomically, the net PM value of those two borrow/lend components is zero. Monetrix reports +1,000 USDC because only the supplied side is included.\n\nThe inflated total propagates through the settlement pipeline:\n\n[`src/core/MonetrixAccountant.sol#L180-L192`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2026-04-monetrix/blob/main/src/core/MonetrixAccountant.sol#L180-L192)\n\n```solidity\nfunction surplus() public view returns (int256) {\n    return totalBackingSigned() - int256(usdm.totalSupply());\n}\n\nfunction distributableSurplus() public view returns (int256) {\n    int256 s = surplus();\n    address re = IMonetrixVaultReader(vault).redeemEscrow();\n    uint256 sf = re == address(0) ? 0 : IRedeemEscrow(re).shortfall();\n    return s - int256(sf);\n}\n```\n\nGate 3 of `settleDailyPnL()` accepts any `proposedYield` up to this inflated `distributableSurplus()`:\n\n[`src/core/MonetrixAccountant.sol#L213-L216`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2026-04-monetrix/blob/main/src/core/MonetrixAccountant.sol#L213-L216)\n\n```solidity\nint256 ds = distributableSurplus();\nrequire(ds > 0, \"Accountant: no distributable surplus\");\ndistributable = uint256(ds);\nrequire(proposedYield <= distributable, \"Accountant: exceeds distributable\");\n```\n\n`MonetrixVault.settle()` then transfers real USDC from the Vault to `YieldEscrow`, and `distributeYield()` mints USDM to sUSDM and routes USDC to the Insurance Fund and Foundation:\n\n[`src/core/MonetrixVault.sol#L364-L414`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2026-04-monetrix/blob/main/src/core/MonetrixVault.sol#L364-L414)\n\nThis is not prevented by the EVM USDC check in `settle()`:\n\n```solidity\nuint256 available = vaultBal > shortfall_ ? vaultBal - shortfall_ : 0;\nrequire(available >= proposedYield, \"insufficient EVM USDC\");\n```\n\nThat check only verifies that the Vault has enough liquid USDC to move into the yield pipeline. It does not verify that the USDC is surplus. If `totalBackingSigned()` is inflated by omitted PM debt, `settle()` can move depositor principal as if it were yield.\n\nThis issue is distinct from treating supplied USDC as immediately bridgeable. That bridge issue concerns `_sendL1Bridge()` and CoreWriter `SEND_ASSET` availability. This finding concerns solvency accounting in `MonetrixAccountant.totalBackingSigned()`: borrow/lend debt is present in the same `0x811` state Monetrix reads, but only the asset side is booked.\n\n## Impact Explanation\n\nThe impact is USDM insolvency through over-distribution of yield.\n\nIn the demonstrated scenario:\n\n1. A user deposits and stakes 1,000,000 USDC, producing 1,000,000 USDM supply.\n2. The Vault has PM borrow/lend state with BTC supply worth 1,000 USDC.\n3. The same PM account has a USDC borrow liability worth 1,000 USDC.\n4. Real backing is exactly equal to USDM supply before settlement.\n5. `totalBackingSigned()` reports 1,001,000 USDC because it counts the BTC supply and ignores the USDC borrow.\n6. `distributableSurplus()` reports 1,000 USDC of phantom surplus.\n7. `settle(1_000e6)` and `distributeYield()` succeed.\n8. `distributeYield()` mints 700 USDM to sUSDM and routes 300 USDC out to Insurance/Foundation under the default 70% / 10% / 20% split.\n9. Reported backing still equals reported USDM supply, but real backing is 1,000 USDC short after subtracting the still-present PM borrow liability.\n\nThe loss is not an accounting display issue only. Real USDC leaves the Vault or is converted into user yield distributions, while the unbooked PM debt remains economically owed by the Vault's PM account. Remaining USDM holders are diluted because USDM supply exceeds real backing.\n\nHigh severity is justified because this breaks the core peg-solvency invariant and allows the protocol to distribute non-existent yield as real value. Gate 4 limits the maximum yield per settlement interval, but it does not eliminate the issue; it only bounds the rate at which omitted PM debt can be converted into phantom yield. The impact scales with the size of omitted borrow liabilities and can recur across settlement cycles while the debt remains unaccounted.\n\n## Likelihood Explanation\n\nThe issue requires Portfolio Margin / borrow-lend state to exist for the Vault or configured multisig PM account. That is a realistic operating condition: Monetrix explicitly integrates the `0x811` PM supplied balance into its backing calculation, and Hyperliquid's Portfolio Margin docs state that eligible assets can be supplied and that insufficient balances can be automatically borrowed against eligible collateral.\n\nThe immediate settlement path is Operator-gated. This does not make the issue purely trusted-operator risk. The protocol deliberately relies on `MonetrixAccountant` Gate 3 to bound an Operator-submitted `proposedYield` by real distributable surplus. The bug is that the on-chain bound is computed from an incomplete PM balance sheet.\n\nThe practical prerequisites are:\n\n1. PM / borrow-lend is active for the relevant L1 account.\n2. The account has supplied collateral that Monetrix registers and counts.\n3. The account also has borrow debt, potentially in a different token.\n4. The Vault has enough EVM USDC liquidity to execute `settle()`.\n5. The Operator submits a `proposedYield` that fits within the inflated Gate 3 and Gate 4 caps.\n\nNo oracle manipulation, reentrancy, unusual token behavior, or transaction ordering is required. The debt can arise from normal Portfolio Margin behavior. Hyperliquid documents that, when placing spot and perp orders under PM, insufficient balance can be automatically borrowed against eligible collateral and borrowed assets accrue interest.\n\nThe main deployment constraint is that Hyperliquid PM has per-user and global caps during alpha/pre-alpha phases. Those caps can limit the maximum omitted borrow debt at a given time, but they do not change the root cause or eliminate the solvency impact for any non-zero borrow liability.\n\n## Recommendation\n\nReplace `suppliedBalance()` with a full borrow/lend state reader that returns both `borrow.value` and `supply.value`.\n\nFor every PM borrowable token that can appear on the Vault or configured multisig PM account:\n\n- add `supply.value` to backing;\n- subtract `borrow.value` from backing;\n- convert non-USDC supplied and borrowed values to USDC using the same conservative oracle/decimal path;\n- include borrow tokens even when the borrowed token is different from the supplied collateral token.\n\nA minimal interface shape would be:\n\n```solidity\nstruct BorrowLendTokenState {\n    uint64 borrowBasis;\n    uint64 borrowValue;\n    uint64 supplyBasis;\n    uint64 supplyValue;\n}\n\nfunction borrowLendState(address account, uint64 token)\n    internal\n    view\n    returns (BorrowLendTokenState memory state)\n{\n    (bool ok, bytes memory res) = HyperCoreConstants.PRECOMPILE_SUPPLIED_BALANCE.staticcall(\n        abi.encode(account, token)\n    );\n    require(ok && res.length >= 128, \"PrecompileReader: borrow/lend read failed\");\n    (state.borrowBasis, state.borrowValue, state.supplyBasis, state.supplyValue) =\n        abi.decode(res, (uint64, uint64, uint64, uint64));\n}\n```\n\nThe Accountant also needs a complete borrow-token registry or explicit borrowable-token list for each PM account it reads. Relying only on the supplied registry is insufficient because a PM account can borrow a different token than the supplied collateral token. At minimum, the list should cover USDC plus every borrowable token enabled for the strategy.\n\nIf Monetrix cannot read a complete PM balance sheet on-chain, the safer mitigation is to disable PM borrow/lend components for yield-settlement backing until an authoritative aggregate PM equity reader is available.\n\nAdd regression tests for:\n\n- supplied collateral with same-token borrow debt;\n- supplied collateral with different-token borrow debt;\n- registered debt token with zero supply and non-zero borrow;\n- `settle()` reverting when apparent surplus exists only because debt was omitted.",
    "proof_of_concept": "The PoC demonstrates the issue against the real implementation code used by the repository's Foundry harness. The in-scope contracts are deployed through the existing C4 `C4Submission` setup, including UUPS proxies. HyperCore read precompiles are mocked because they are chain precompiles and are not available in local Foundry tests; the mock returns the exact ABI layout used by Hyperliquid's `0x811` borrow/lend precompile.\n\nThe test strengthens the registry case by registering both:\n\n- BTC, the supplied collateral token; and\n- USDC, the borrow debt token.\n\nEven when USDC is present in the Accountant's supplied registry, the debt is ignored because `PrecompileReader.suppliedBalance()` only decodes the fourth ABI word (`supply.value`) and discards `borrow.value`.\n\nPlace the following file at `test/c4/PmBorrowLiabilitiesBacking.t.sol`:\n\n```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.27;\n\nimport \"./C4Submission.t.sol\";\n\ncontract PmBorrowLiabilitiesBackingPoC is C4Submission {\n    function test_pmBorrowLiabilityInflatesBackingAndDistributesPhantomYield() public {\n        uint256 depositAmount = 1_000_000e6;\n        uint256 omittedBorrowDebt = 1_000e6;\n        uint64 omittedBorrowDebtL1 = uint64(omittedBorrowDebt * 100);\n\n        uint32 btcPerpIndex = 0;\n        uint32 btcSpotToken = 197;\n        uint32 btcSpotPairAssetId = 10142;\n        uint64 btcSuppliedL1 = 1_000_000; // 0.01 BTC at 8 decimals\n        uint64 btcOraclePx = 1_000_000;   // $100,000 with BTC szDecimals=5\n\n        _deposit(user1, depositAmount);\n        _stake(user1, depositAmount);\n\n        vm.prank(admin);\n        config.addTradeableAsset(\n            MonetrixConfig.TradeableAsset({\n                perpIndex: btcPerpIndex,\n                spotIndex: btcSpotToken,\n                spotPairAssetId: btcSpotPairAssetId\n            })\n        );\n\n        vm.prank(admin);\n        vault.setPmEnabled(true);\n\n        vm.prank